::ENG4_U01_UA_Q01::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q01
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Independent learning does not mean working without support. Effective independent learners use resources deliberately, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise their work. What is the central claim? {
~A. Independent learning requires avoiding support.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. Feedback automatically improves work.#This is too broad.
=C. Independent learning combines responsibility with thoughtful use of support.#Correct. This captures the central claim.
~D. Revision is unnecessary for independent learners.#This contradicts the passage.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q02::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q02
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Effective independent learners use resources deliberately, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise their work. What does revise mean? {
~A. Ignore#This is the opposite.
~B. Copy without thought#This is not revision.
~C. Finish without checking#This is not revision.
=D. Review and improve#Correct. Revise means review and improve.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q03::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q03
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A student who receives feedback but does not examine it carefully may repeat the same error, while a student who studies the feedback can identify the pattern behind the mistake. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Feedback always improves work automatically.#This ignores the condition.
=B. Feedback helps most when students study it closely enough to recognize patterns in their mistakes.#Correct. This preserves the contrast.
~C. Students should avoid feedback because it causes errors.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. Studying feedback makes students repeat the same error.#This reverses the meaning.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q04::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q04
[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Effective independent learners use resources deliberately, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise their work. Which sentence is punctuated correctly? {
=A. Learners use resources deliberately, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise their work.#Correct. The series is clear.
~B. Learners use resources, deliberately ask focused questions evaluate feedback and revise their work.#The punctuation blurs the series.
~C. Learners use resources deliberately ask, focused questions, evaluate feedback and revise their work.#The comma is misplaced.
~D. Learners, use resources deliberately, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise their work.#The first comma incorrectly separates subject and verb.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q05::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q05
[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: Strong independent learning therefore depends on both personal responsibility and thoughtful use of available support. Which oral explanation is most coherent? {
~A. Independent learning is support and responsibility and stuff.#This is vague.
~B. Support is bad because independent means alone.#This contradicts the passage.
~C. Responsibility means never asking questions.#This contradicts the passage.
=D. The passage argues that independent learners must take responsibility while also using support thoughtfully.#Correct. This is coherent and accurate.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q06::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q06
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Effective independent learners use resources deliberately, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise their work. What does focused mean? {
=A. Specific and directed#Correct. Focused questions are specific.
~B. Random#This is the opposite.
~C. Decorative#This is unrelated.
~D. Avoided#This is unrelated.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q07::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q07
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Independent learning does not mean working without support. Which paraphrase distorts the source? {
~A. Independent learning can include support.#This is accurate.
~B. Independent learning is not the same as isolation.#This is accurate.
~C. Independent learners may ask focused questions.#This is accurate.
=D. Independent learning means refusing support.#Correct. This contradicts the source.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q08::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q08
[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: A student who studies the feedback can identify the pattern behind the mistake. Which sentence follows standard conventions? {
~A. A student who study feedback can identify patterns.#Subject-verb agreement is incorrect.
~B. A student who studies feedback can identifies patterns.#The verb form is incorrect.
=C. A student who studies feedback can identify patterns.#Correct. Subject and verb agree.
~D. a student who studies feedback can identify patterns.#The sentence begins with a lowercase letter.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q09::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q09
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: In college and workplace settings, this habit matters because people are expected to improve work from evidence, feedback, and reflection. Which claim is supported? {
~A. Evidence and feedback are unnecessary outside school.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. Reflection prevents people from improving.#This contradicts the passage.
~C. People are never expected to improve work.#This contradicts the passage.
=D. The habit of improving from feedback supports college and workplace readiness.#Correct. This follows the final sentence.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q10::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q10
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: In college and workplace settings, this habit matters because people are expected to improve work from evidence, feedback, and reflection. What does reflection mean here? {
~A. Light bouncing from a surface#This is the wrong context.
~B. A spelling rule#This is unrelated.
=C. Careful thought about work and improvement#Correct. Reflection means thoughtful review.
~D. A public speech#This is unrelated.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q11::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q11
[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: Effective independent learners use resources deliberately, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise their work. Which presentation order is strongest? {
~A. Random example, unrelated opinion, claim#This lacks organization.
~B. Vocabulary only, no claim#This is incomplete.
~C. Conclusion first, no evidence#This is weak.
=D. Claim, paraphrased evidence, rationale, conclusion#Correct. This is coherent.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q12::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q12
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A student who receives feedback but does not examine it carefully may repeat the same error. What is the function of this detail? {
=A. It shows why students must evaluate feedback instead of only receiving it.#Correct. It supports the passage's claim.
~B. It proves feedback is useless.#This overstates the detail.
~C. It says students should ignore mistakes.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. It introduces an unrelated example.#It is directly related.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q13::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q13
[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Strong independent learning therefore depends on both personal responsibility and thoughtful use of available support. Which sentence is clear and conventional? {
~A. Strong independent learning depend on responsibility and support.#Subject-verb agreement is incorrect.
~B. Strong independent learning, depends on responsibility and support.#The comma separates subject and verb.
=C. Strong independent learning depends on responsibility and thoughtful use of support.#Correct. This is clear and conventional.
~D. strong independent learning depends on responsibility and support.#The sentence begins with a lowercase letter.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q14::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q14
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Strong independent learning therefore depends on both personal responsibility and thoughtful use of available support. What does depends on mean? {
~A. Rejects#This is the opposite.
~B. Decorates#This is unrelated.
~C. Copies#This is unrelated.
=D. Relies on#Correct. Depends on means relies on.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q15::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q15
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Effective independent learners use resources deliberately, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise their work. Which paraphrase is most complete? {
~A. Independent learners use resources.#This is too incomplete.
=B. Effective independent learners intentionally use resources, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise.#Correct. This preserves the full list.
~C. Independent learners only revise work.#This is too narrow.
~D. Independent learners avoid resources and questions.#This contradicts the passage.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q16::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q16
[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: In college and workplace settings, this habit matters because people are expected to improve work from evidence, feedback, and reflection. Which oral explanation uses academic vocabulary effectively? {
=A. This habit supports readiness because college and workplace audiences expect improvement through evidence, feedback, and reflection.#Correct. This is precise and coherent.
~B. This habit is good because work stuff matters.#This is vague.
~C. This habit is obviously perfect everywhere.#This overstates the claim.
~D. Evidence is feedback and reflection is evidence.#This confuses terms.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q17::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q17
[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: In college and workplace settings, this habit matters because people are expected to improve work from evidence, feedback, and reflection. Which sentence punctuates the series correctly? {
~A. People improve work from evidence feedback and reflection.#Commas are missing.
~B. People improve work, from evidence feedback, and reflection.#The commas are misplaced.
=C. People improve work from evidence, feedback, and reflection.#Correct. The series is clear.
~D. People, improve work from evidence, feedback, and reflection.#The first comma is incorrect.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q18::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q18
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Independent learning does not mean working without support. Effective independent learners use resources deliberately, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise their work. Which answer best combines both sentences? {
~A. Independent learning means never using resources.#This contradicts the passage.
=B. Independent learning includes responsible use of support such as resources, questions, feedback, and revision.#Correct. This synthesizes both sentences.
~C. Effective learners only ask questions but never revise.#This is incomplete and inaccurate.
~D. Support prevents independent learning.#This contradicts the passage.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q19::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q19
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: A student who studies the feedback can identify the pattern behind the mistake. What does pattern mean here? {
=A. A repeated form or recurring issue#Correct. A pattern is a repeated issue.
~B. A decorative design#This is the wrong context.
~C. A complete sentence#This is unrelated.
~D. A public audience#This is unrelated.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q20::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q20
[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: Strong independent learning therefore depends on both personal responsibility and thoughtful use of available support. Which phrase should be emphasized in an oral explanation? {
~A. Only personal responsibility#This omits support.
=B. Both personal responsibility and thoughtful use of support#Correct. This preserves the balanced claim.
~C. Only available support#This omits responsibility.
~D. Neither support nor responsibility#This contradicts the passage.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q21::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q21
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A student who receives feedback but does not examine it carefully may repeat the same error, while a student who studies the feedback can identify the pattern behind the mistake. What relationship is shown? {
=A. Contrast#Correct. It contrasts two ways of responding to feedback.
~B. Chronology only#The sentence is not mainly time order.
~C. Definition of college#This is not a definition.
~D. Unrelated examples#The examples are related.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q22::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q22
[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Effective independent learners use resources deliberately, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise their work. Which revision fixes the error? Original: Effective independent learners uses resources deliberately. {
~A. Effective independent learners uses resources deliberately.#The error remains.
=B. Effective independent learners use resources deliberately.#Correct. The plural subject takes use.
~C. Effective independent learner use resources deliberately.#The singular subject and verb do not agree.
~D. Effective independent learners using resources deliberately.#This is a fragment.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q23::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q23
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Effective independent learners use resources deliberately, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise their work. Which vocabulary choice is most precise for "ask focused questions"? {
=A. Ask specific, purposeful questions#Correct. This preserves focused.
~B. Ask random questions#This changes the meaning.
~C. Ask no questions#This contradicts the passage.
~D. Ask decorative questions#This is unrelated.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q24::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q24
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: In college and workplace settings, this habit matters because people are expected to improve work from evidence, feedback, and reflection. Why does the author include college and workplace settings? {
~A. To say independent learning ends after high school#This contradicts the passage.
=B. To show the practical importance of improving from evidence and feedback#Correct. This explains the readiness connection.
~C. To suggest feedback is only useful at work#This is too narrow.
~D. To change the topic away from learning#This is not the function.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q25::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q25
[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: Independent learning does not mean working without support. Effective independent learners use resources deliberately, ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise their work. Which response best demonstrates Unit 1 synthesis? {
~A. Independent learning means no one helps you.#This contradicts the passage.
~B. Feedback is a word in the passage, and revision is also a word.#This identifies words but does not synthesize.
=C. Independent learning means using support responsibly: learners ask focused questions, evaluate feedback, and revise based on evidence.#Correct. This combines paraphrase, vocabulary, and coherent explanation.
~D. Students should work alone because support is not independent.#This contradicts the passage.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q26::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q26
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Careful reading is not the same as slow reading. A careful reader notices how each sentence changes or refines the idea before it. When a writer qualifies a claim with words such as however, although, or specifically, the reader must preserve that qualification in a paraphrase. A strong paraphrase does more than replace words with synonyms; it restates the full meaning, including relationships among ideas, limits on claims, and the author's emphasis. In college and workplace settings, inaccurate paraphrase can distort a source, weaken an argument, or create confusion about what a document actually says. Which paraphrase best preserves the first sentence? {
~A. Careful reading always requires students to read slowly.#This changes the meaning by turning a contrast into a rule about speed.
~B. Slow reading is the best way to understand all academic texts.#This adds a claim the passage does not make.
~C. Careful reading and slow reading are basically identical.#This contradicts the passage.
=D. Careful reading involves attention to meaning rather than simply reading at a slower pace.#Correct. This preserves the contrast between careful reading and slow reading.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q27::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q27
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Careful reading is not the same as slow reading. A careful reader notices how each sentence changes or refines the idea before it. When a writer qualifies a claim with words such as however, although, or specifically, the reader must preserve that qualification in a paraphrase. A strong paraphrase does more than replace words with synonyms; it restates the full meaning, including relationships among ideas, limits on claims, and the author's emphasis. In college and workplace settings, inaccurate paraphrase can distort a source, weaken an argument, or create confusion about what a document actually says. What must a paraphrase preserve when a writer uses words such as however or although? {
~A. The exact sentence length of the original#Sentence length does not determine whether meaning is preserved.
=B. The qualification or limit placed on the claim#Correct. Words such as however and although often narrow or qualify claims.
~C. Only the vocabulary words that sound academic#Academic vocabulary matters, but the question asks about qualification.
~D. The writer's personal background#The passage does not discuss the writer's background.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q28::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q28
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Careful reading is not the same as slow reading. A careful reader notices how each sentence changes or refines the idea before it. When a writer qualifies a claim with words such as however, although, or specifically, the reader must preserve that qualification in a paraphrase. A strong paraphrase does more than replace words with synonyms; it restates the full meaning, including relationships among ideas, limits on claims, and the author's emphasis. In college and workplace settings, inaccurate paraphrase can distort a source, weaken an argument, or create confusion about what a document actually says. In this passage, what does distort most nearly mean? {
=A. Change in a way that misrepresents meaning#Correct. The passage warns that inaccurate paraphrase can misrepresent a source.
~B. Make a passage shorter#Shortening is summarizing, not necessarily distorting.
~C. Read a document more slowly#The word refers to changed meaning, not reading speed.
~D. Quote a source exactly#Exact quotation is not distortion.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q29::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q29
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: When people discuss a public issue, precision matters. A speaker who describes a policy as beneficial is making a broad positive judgment, but a speaker who calls it equitable is making a more specific claim about fairness. Likewise, describing evidence as relevant means it connects to the issue, while describing it as credible means it can be trusted. Academic vocabulary allows writers and speakers to make exact distinctions. Which word best describes a policy that is fair across groups? {
=A. Equitable#Correct. Equitable means fair and just.
~B. Relevant#Relevant means connected to the issue, not fair.
~C. Credible#Credible means trustworthy, not fair.
~D. Distorted#Distorted means misrepresented.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q30::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q30
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: When people discuss a public issue, precision matters. A speaker who describes a policy as beneficial is making a broad positive judgment, but a speaker who calls it equitable is making a more specific claim about fairness. Likewise, describing evidence as relevant means it connects to the issue, while describing it as credible means it can be trusted. Which word best describes evidence that connects directly to the issue? {
~A. Equitable#Equitable refers to fairness.
~B. Beneficial#Beneficial means helpful or advantageous.
=C. Relevant#Correct. Relevant evidence connects directly to the issue or claim.
~D. Precise#Precise means exact, but the passage uses relevant for connection.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q31::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q31
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A community college review found that students with reliable evening transportation were more likely to attend tutoring sessions, library workshops, and study groups after work. The report did not claim that transportation alone caused higher grades. Instead, it concluded that dependable transit expanded students' access to academic support services. The strongest evidence came from attendance records showing that late-afternoon workshop participation increased after the college added a shuttle route to nearby bus stops. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
~A. The shuttle caused students to earn higher grades.#This overstates the source.
=B. The shuttle appeared to expand access to support services because workshop attendance increased after the route was added.#Correct. This preserves the evidence and avoids exaggeration.
~C. Students stopped needing tutoring once the shuttle was added.#The passage does not say this.
~D. Transportation was the only barrier students faced.#The passage says other barriers should be studied.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q32::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q32
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: A community college review found that students with reliable evening transportation were more likely to attend tutoring sessions, library workshops, and study groups after work. Which word best describes evidence that directly supports a claim? {
=A. Relevant#Correct. Relevant evidence directly connects to a claim.
~B. Decorative#Decorative does not describe evidence quality.
~C. Inflated#Inflated suggests exaggeration.
~D. Vague#Vague evidence is not directly useful.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q33::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q33
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: A modern library is no longer only a storage place for printed books. In many communities, the library functions as a learning center where people attend workshops, use technology, meet with tutors, and access quiet study space. This expanded role does not make books less important; instead, it shows that reading, research, and community learning now support one another. Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
~A. Modern libraries have replaced books with technology.#This distorts the passage.
~B. Libraries are only storage places for printed books.#This contradicts the passage.
~C. Libraries no longer support reading.#This contradicts the passage.
=D. Modern libraries still value books but also support learning through services, tools, and spaces.#Correct. This preserves the balance.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q34::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q34
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: A strong library program balances access to texts with access to guidance, tools, and collaborative spaces. Which word best describes the sentence's academic focus? {
~A. Entertainment#The sentence focuses on learning support, not entertainment.
=B. Balance#Correct. The sentence emphasizes balancing different types of access.
~C. Isolation#The sentence includes collaborative spaces.
~D. Replacement#The passage does not say one access type replaces another.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q35::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q35
[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: In professional communication, conventions are not decorative rules; they protect meaning. A misplaced comma can blur the relationship between ideas, and a sentence fragment can make a recommendation sound unfinished. Which option corrects this fragment? Because conventions protect meaning. {
=A. Conventions protect meaning because they help readers follow reasoning.#Correct. This is a complete sentence.
~B. Because conventions protect meaning.#This remains a fragment.
~C. Because conventions, protect meaning.#This remains incomplete and adds an incorrect comma.
~D. Conventions, because protect meaning.#This is not standard English.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q36::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q36
[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Passage: Clear conventions also help readers evaluate a writer's reasoning. Which sentence has correct subject-verb agreement? {
~A. Clear conventions helps readers evaluate reasoning.#Conventions is plural, so helps is incorrect.
~B. Clear convention help readers evaluate reasoning.#Convention is singular, so help is incorrect.
~C. Clear conventions helping readers evaluate reasoning.#This is a fragment.
=D. Clear conventions help readers evaluate reasoning.#Correct. The plural subject matches the verb help.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q37::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q37
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Before changing the schedule for a public study center, the planning team gathered feedback from students, tutors, and local employers. The comments did not produce complete consensus, but they revealed a shared concern: evening access mattered for students who worked during the day. What does consensus mean? {
~A. A single recommendation#This is not consensus.
=B. General agreement#Correct. Consensus means general agreement.
~C. A stakeholder group#This names affected people, not agreement.
~D. A trial period#This is a testing period.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q38::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q38
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: The team recommended extending hours twice a week and reviewing attendance data after three months. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. The team suggested trying later hours two times each week and checking attendance after three months.#Correct. This preserves the recommendation.
~B. The team permanently changed the schedule every night.#This overstates the recommendation.
~C. The team rejected all schedule changes.#This contradicts the passage.
~D. The team studied attendance before making any recommendation.#The passage says attendance would be reviewed after three months.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q39::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q39
[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: Digital tools can expand learning when students use them with purpose. A note-taking application can help a student organize source ideas, and a shared document can make peer feedback more efficient. However, technology does not automatically improve learning. If students copy information without paraphrasing, accept suggestions without evaluating them, or use digital tools to avoid careful thinking, the tool weakens rather than strengthens academic work. Which paraphrase preserves the passage's qualification? {
~A. Technology always improves learning.#This ignores the qualification.
~B. Technology always weakens academic work.#This ignores the positive condition.
~C. Students should avoid all digital tools.#This distorts the passage.
=D. Technology can support learning when students use it purposefully and keep thinking carefully.#Correct. This preserves the condition.
}

::ENG4_U01_UA_Q40::
Question ID: ENG4_U01_UA_Q40
[MLA.ENG4.V.01] Passage: Responsible technology use requires students to preserve source meaning, evaluate support, and communicate conclusions clearly. What does preserve mean here? {
~A. Remove#This is the opposite.
=B. Keep accurately#Correct. Students must keep source meaning accurate.
~C. Decorate#This is unrelated.
~D. Guess quickly#This is unrelated.
}
